# A block comment sitting inside an indentation region, which is the one place
# scala's two seated rows have to agree with each other.
#
# `_indent/.offside/.slashes` decides where the template body ends; a comment
# is not code and must not close it. `block_comment/.marrow/.kotlin_block`
# decides where the comment ends; get that wrong and the offside reader is
# handed operator tokens at a column it has to rule on. The two claims below
# are one claim each, and they fall together:
#
#   sc-base  both hold        template_body [8, 41)  block_comment [10, 17)
#   sc-r0    comment survives, template_body gone
#   sc-r4    comment gone,     template_body gone
#
# That last line is the whole reason this file exists. The comment row is not
# merely co-located with the offside row in one corpus fixture - un-seating it
# costs the offside construct as well, inside a file of 42 bytes where fixture
# layout cannot be the explanation. `research/joinery/consort/RESULT-3-scala.md`
# read a +5,500 corpus residual as those two rows "cooperating" off a tree that
# had regressed; the corpus can no longer be the only evidence either way.
roots 1
mends 0
holds template_body
spans template_body 8 41
holds block_comment
spans block_comment 10 17
